<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>DiscoveryOS — Dana Kreisberg</title><meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter+Tight:ital,wght@0,300;0,400;0,500;1,100;1,300;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"><style is:global>
:root{--paper:#fff;--paper-2:#f2f2f2;--ink:oklch(14% .01 240);--ink-60:oklch(14% .01 240/.62);--ink-40:oklch(14% .01 240/.42);--rule:oklch(14% .01 240/.14);--display:"Inter Tight",sans-serif;--body:"Inter",sans-serif;--mono:"JetBrains Mono",monospace;--gutter:8%;--bagel-purple:#5C5CEB}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--body);-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}img{display:block;max-width:100%}p,h1,h2,h3{margin:0}.meta,.step-num,.decision-mono,.exploration-kicker{font:11px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-40)}.title-section{padding:64px var(--gutter) 56px}.title-meta{margin-bottom:40px}.cs-h1{font:400 clamp(48px,7vw,96px)/.95 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.04em}.cs-h1 em{font-weight:100;font-style:italic;color:var(--bagel-purple)}.lede{max-width:62ch;margin-top:40px;color:var(--ink-60);font-size:19px;line-height:1.5}.see-in-action-link{display:inline-block;margin-top:16px;color:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:500}.meta-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:32px;margin-top:64px;padding-top:24px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}.meta-val{margin-top:8px;font-size:15px}.section{padding:96px var(--gutter)}.section-alt{padding:100px var(--gutter);background:var(--paper-2);border-block:1px solid var(--rule)}.cs-h2{max-width:16ch;font:400 clamp(36px,3.8vw,60px)/1.02 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.03em}.cs-h2-wide{max-width:23ch;margin-top:16px;font:400 clamp(40px,4.4vw,72px)/1.02 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.032em}.body-text{max-width:58ch;color:var(--ink-60);font-size:16px;line-height:1.65}.body-text+.body-text{margin-top:16px}.two-col,.feat-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.1fr;gap:64px;align-items:start}.feat-split{grid-template-columns:1fr 1.4fr;gap:56px}.img-frame{display:flex;flex-direction:column}.img-frame img{width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px}.fig-cap{padding-top:10px;color:var(--ink-40);font-size:13px;line-height:1.45}.stat-row,.outcomes-bold{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);margin-top:56px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}.stat{padding:40px 32px 40px 0}.stat-n{padding-top:24px;font:400 clamp(64px,8vw,128px)/1 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.04em}.stat-d{font-size:.45em;color:var(--ink-40)}.stat-l{max-width:27ch;margin-top:18px;color:var(--ink-60);font-size:15px;line-height:1.5}.reframe-statement{max-width:25ch;margin-top:48px;font:clamp(2rem,4vw,4rem)/1.03 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.035em}.act-divider{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:24px;padding:56px var(--gutter) 48px;border-block:1px solid var(--rule)}.act-num{font:10px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-40)}.act-title{font:300 clamp(2.4rem,6vw,5.2rem)/1.04 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.03em}.workflow-line{margin-top:32px;color:var(--bagel-purple);font:clamp(1.35rem,2.5vw,2.2rem)/1.25 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.025em}.exploration-grid,.walkthrough-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:24px;margin-top:48px}.exploration-card{padding:28px;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;background:var(--paper)}.exploration-card .img-frame,.walkthrough-step .img-frame{margin-top:24px}.exploration-title,.walkthrough-title{margin-top:12px;font:400 clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.25rem)/1.05 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.025em}.walkthrough-step{padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}.outcome-item{padding:32px;border-right:1px solid var(--rule)}.outcome-item:first-child{padding-left:0}.outcome-item:last-child{border:0}.outcome-stat{color:var(--bagel-purple);font:300 clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)/1 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.03em}.outcome-label{max-width:22ch;margin-top:12px;color:var(--ink-60);font-size:14px;line-height:1.5}.outcome-footnote{margin-top:20px;color:var(--ink-40);font-size:12px;font-style:italic}.closing{padding:140px var(--gutter);color:#e6e6e7;background:#0b0b0d}.closing-kicker{font:11px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#ffffff80}.closing-quote{max-width:22ch;margin-top:32px;font:300 clamp(32px,4.4vw,72px)/1.1 var(--display);letter-spacing:-.03em}.closing-quote em{font-style:italic}@media(max-width:900px){:root{--gutter:5%}.two-col,.feat-split,.exploration-grid,.walkthrough-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:40px}.stat-row,.outcomes-bold,.meta-row{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}}@media(max-width:600px){:root{--gutter:20px}.section,.section-alt{padding-block:72px}.stat-row,.outcomes-bold,.meta-row{grid-template-columns:1fr}.outcome-item,.outcome-item:first-child{padding:28px 0;border-right:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}.act-divider{gap:14px;padding-block:40px}}
</style></head><body><Nav />
<section class="title-section"><div class="title-meta"><span class="meta">Case Study · Bagel.AI</span></div><h1 class="cs-h1">Discovery<em>OS</em></h1><p class="lede">Bagel.AI surfaces customer evidence for product teams. Discovery was the most powerful space in the product — and the least used. We redesigned it from the ground up.</p><a href="https://bagel.ai/platform-overview/bagel-discovery-os/#See-it-in-action" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="see-in-action-link">See it in action ↗</a><div class="meta-row"><div><span class="meta">Role</span><div class="meta-val">UX Design &amp; Strategy</div></div><div><span class="meta">Scope</span><div class="meta-val">End-to-end product redesign</div></div><div><span class="meta">Year</span><div class="meta-val">2025 · NDA</div></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="two-col"><div><span class="meta">01 · Context</span><h2 class="cs-h2" style="margin-top:16px">The product had the data. The problem was the workflow.</h2></div><div><p class="body-text">Bagel aggregates customer signals from calls, support, CRM, and feature requests, then uses AI to surface opportunities for product teams. The challenge was never a lack of evidence — it was helping PMs turn scattered signals into something worth investigating and acting on.</p><p class="body-text">The same evidence appeared across Discovery, Trends, Ideas, and Customer 360. Each space had value on its own, but there was no coherent path between them.</p></div></div><div class="img-frame" style="margin-top:48px"><img src="/images/bagel-research-space-overlap.png" alt="Four Bagel product spaces drawing from the same evidence pool"><div class="fig-cap">Four spaces. One evidence pool. No coherent path for a PM to follow.</div></div></section>
<section class="section-alt"><span class="meta">02 · Evidence</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">PMs were exploring — but rarely progressing to product decisions.</h2><p class="body-text">Discovery queries were often broad and exploratory rather than precise searches. The data showed interest in the evidence, but a weak bridge from exploration into an idea or product decision.</p><div class="stat-row"><div class="stat"><div class="stat-n">248</div><div class="stat-l">Total Discovery queries across all accounts</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-n">4.4<span class="stat-d">%</span></div><div class="stat-l">Of queries converted to an idea — the intended next action</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-n">~140</div><div class="stat-l">Average weekly Idea page views, compared with near-zero Discovery usage</div></div></div><p class="reframe-statement">The problem wasn’t a missing feature. It was a missing workflow.</p><p class="meta" style="margin-top:56px">Before — the old product</p><div class="img-frame" style="margin-top:12px"><img src="/images/bagel-02.jpg" alt="Old Discovery space — blank search prompt, no path to action"><div class="fig-cap">The old Discovery space — a blank search prompt with no starting point and no path to action.</div></div></section>
<div class="act-divider" style="background:var(--paper-2)"><span class="act-num">Part 01</span><h2 class="act-title">Research</h2></div><section class="section-alt"><span class="meta">03 · Workflow mapping</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">Understanding the PM decision-making workflow.</h2><p class="body-text">We mapped how PMs move from a customer signal to a decision — not simply how they use Bagel. This made it possible to see where existing capabilities helped, and where the product left the work unfinished.</p><div class="workflow-line">Gather signals → Investigate → Make sense → Prioritize → Build roadmap → Measure &amp; learn</div><div class="img-frame" style="margin-top:48px"><img src="/images/bagel-research-mental-model_simplified.png" alt="Simplified PM mental model and jobs to be done"><div class="fig-cap">The decision-making workflow, mapped through three jobs: explore, investigate, and measure.</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="meta">04 · Exploration</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">Testing the boundaries.</h2><p class="body-text">Before defining the new product, we tested two directions at either end of the problem space. Both clarified what Bagel should — and should not — become.</p><div class="exploration-grid"><article class="exploration-card"><span class="exploration-kicker">Direction 01</span><h3 class="exploration-title">Improve the existing Discovery workflow</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">Could a more useful, actionable Discovery experience solve the problem? It improved the existing interaction, but it still left the fundamental question unanswered: where do I start?</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-v1-frame-02.png" alt="Exploration of an improved Discovery workflow"></div></article><article class="exploration-card"><span class="exploration-kicker">Direction 02</span><h3 class="exploration-title">Could Bagel become a planning tool?</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">We also explored a broader roadmap and product-planning role. PMs already rely on dedicated planning tools, and Bagel lacked the wider business context needed to replace them.</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-v2-frame-06.png" alt="Exploration of a broader product planning direction"></div></article></div><p class="reframe-statement" style="margin-top:64px">The opportunity was somewhere in between.</p></section>
<div class="act-divider"><span class="act-num">Part 02</span><h2 class="act-title">DiscoveryOS</h2></div><section class="section-alt" style="background:#fff"><span class="meta">05 · Solution</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">One place to start every investigation.</h2><p class="body-text">Instead of another search experience or another planning tool, DiscoveryOS gives PMs a natural starting point for the evidence Bagel already contains — and a continuous path from question to action.</p><div class="img-frame" style="margin-top:48px"><img src="/images/bagel-01.png" alt="DiscoveryOS starting point"></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="meta">06 · Product workflow</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">From a question to evidence — without leaving the flow.</h2><div class="workflow-line">Ask naturally → Surface opportunities → Explore themes → Validate with evidence</div><div class="walkthrough-grid"><article class="walkthrough-step"><span class="step-num">01 · Ask naturally</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Start with the question you have.</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:12px">PMs can begin naturally, without needing to know the exact search terms or destination first.</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-01.png" alt="Natural-language DiscoveryOS starting point"></div></article><article class="walkthrough-step"><span class="step-num">02 · Surface opportunities</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Turn a question into themes worth exploring.</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:12px">The system surfaces opportunities from the evidence, providing a useful first structure for investigation.</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-04.png" alt="Opportunities surfaced from customer evidence"></div></article><article class="walkthrough-step"><span class="step-num">03 · Explore themes</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Go deeper through hierarchy.</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:12px">Themes unfold into insights and evidence through progressive disclosure rather than a trail of separate pages.</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-04-hierarchy2.png" alt="Hierarchy from opportunity to insight and evidence"></div></article><article class="walkthrough-step"><span class="step-num">04 · Validate with evidence</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Make the evidence part of the decision.</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:12px">The final insight remains connected to the signals behind it, so a PM can validate, enrich, or act without switching context.</p><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-10.png" alt="Evidence-backed DiscoveryOS insight"></div></article></div></section>
<section class="section-alt"><span class="meta">07 · Design decisions</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">Designing for momentum, not navigation.</h2><div class="feat-split" style="margin-top:48px"><div><span class="decision-mono">Decision 01</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Hierarchy over navigation</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">Question → opportunity → insight. Information unfolds in layers, so people can move deeper without losing the context that brought them there.</p></div><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-04-hierarchy.png" alt="Hierarchy in the DiscoveryOS insight view"></div></div><div class="feat-split" style="margin-top:80px"><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/bagel-05.png" alt="Insight-level actions in context"></div><div><span class="decision-mono">Decision 02</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Stay in context</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">Creating an idea, adding findings, asking a follow-up question, or investigating evidence all happen in the same workflow — not through a separate destination.</p></div></div><div class="feat-split" style="margin-top:80px"><div><span class="decision-mono">Decision 03</span><h3 class="walkthrough-title">Living artifacts</h3><p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">An insight is not a static AI output. Teams can shape it, add widgets, ask follow-ups, and keep it useful as new evidence arrives.</p></div><div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/Bagel-08c@2x.png" alt="Customizable living artifact in DiscoveryOS"></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="meta">08 · Outcome</span><h2 class="cs-h2-wide">From disconnected spaces to one workflow.</h2><p class="body-text">DiscoveryOS unified exploration, validation, and action into a single workflow. It was customer-validated and became a central way to demonstrate Bagel’s product story.</p><div class="img-frame" style="margin-top:48px"><img src="/images/bagel-10.png" alt="DiscoveryOS shipped experience"></div><div class="outcomes-bold"><div class="outcome-item"><div class="outcome-stat">1 flow</div><div class="outcome-label">Four disconnected entry points became one continuous discovery workflow</div></div><div class="outcome-item"><div class="outcome-stat">Validated</div><div class="outcome-label">Prototypes became a centerpiece of enterprise demos and prospect walkthroughs</div></div><div class="outcome-item"><div class="outcome-stat">5× growth</div><div class="outcome-label">Enterprise customer-base growth within three months of launch*</div></div></div><p class="outcome-footnote">*Enterprise growth was shared by the founders following launch. Multiple factors, including the founders and broader business context, contributed to this outcome.</p></section>
<section class="closing"><div class="closing-kicker">Final learning</div><p class="closing-quote">AI should be a collaborator, not an educator.<br><em>It should adapt to existing workflows, not change them.</em></p></section>
</body></html>
